<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import SupportRoom from './lib/SupportRoom.abide'
import supportRoomSource from './lib/SupportRoom.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <p>Nothing on the page polls and nothing on it opens a connection. A page that reads the
    <code>GET</code> follows it, and a <code>patch</code> on the server reaches every page following
    it.</p>

    <Section title="Live state is three declarations">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'reactive(fn, { global: true })'}</code></td><td>the value every caller shares, one per args key where <code>fn</code> declares a parameter</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const room = GET(rooms)</code></td><td>its address — a browser reading it follows it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>const say = POST(…)</code></td><td>the way in, a handler that patches the value</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>global: true</code> puts the value in one process-wide cache that outlives every
        request (11.34). Leave it out and the value is request-local on a server (11.32), so every
        caller gets one of their own and nobody hears anybody. Every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/transports')}>Transports</a> reference, and what a channel is
        before it has an address is
        <a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A turn one tab sends reaches every tab">
        <Example
            id="socket-room"
            title="`GET` + `POST` — your room, every tab"
            summary="A support room per browser: a `GET` every tab follows and a `POST` that adds a turn. Open this page in a second tab, send from either, and the last turn changes in both — and in nobody else's."
            files={{
                'lib/SupportRoom.abide': supportRoomSource,
            }}
        >
            <SupportRoom />
        </Example>

        <p><em>2 handlers, 0 frames</em> — against the arm, which opens the socket, frames each
        turn, holds a turn sent while it is down, keeps a latest turn per room, fans each turn out
        to that room's tabs and reopens the connection when it drops.</p>

        <p>Each reader gets a room of their own. The body declares a parameter, so
        <code>rooms</code> is keyed (11.2) and <code>{'room({ id })'}</code> is one room per id. The
        id is a cookie that every tab of one browser sends and nobody else's does, so the room is
        yours and your tabs'.</p>

        <p>A browser reading a <code>GET</code> is subscribed to that handler's productions for
        those args (18.3), over one web socket mux every followed <code>GET</code> on the page
        shares (18.1). So a page following four rooms holds one connection.</p>

        <p>The tab that sends does not paint its own turn. <code>say</code> sends the text, the
        handler patches the room, and what the patch wrote goes to every follower (18.11), this tab
        included. The transcript is the server's, in the order the server took it, in every tab at
        once.</p>

        <p>Each turn carries an id the server gives it, and the room declares <code>by: 'id'</code>.
        A follower's copy carries <code>by</code> (18.4), so a <code>{'{#for turn of room}'}</code>
        keys its rows by it (32.32) and a new turn adds one row. <code>ttl</code> is the life of a
        keyed entry (11.45), which is how a room nobody uses is let go.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The server writes each message, so nobody forges its author">
        <p><code>say</code> takes <code>{'{ id, text }'}</code> and nothing else. The turn's id, its
        speaker and anything else it carries are written by the handler, so a caller can only ever
        add a turn that says what they typed. The example writes <code>'you'</code>; an app with
        sign-in writes who is signed in:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`const say = POST(({ id, text }: { id: string; text: string }) => {
    const { name } = principal.resolved()
    rooms({ id }).patch((turns) => {
        turns.push({ id: crypto.randomUUID(), speaker: name, text })
    })
})`} />

        <p>The handler may read <code>principal</code>, because a handler is bound to the request it
        answers (16.25). The room's own body may not: <code>global: true</code> is a build error on
        a body reading <code>principal</code> (11.37), since one value serves every caller.</p>

        <p>So the room is append-only with no rung saying so. A caller has no way to write to it
        except the handlers you declared, and each one writes exactly what its body writes. Who may
        post is the <code>POST</code>'s own <code>middleware</code>, which runs on every call
        (16.11). A moderator removing a turn is a second <code>POST</code>, behind a rung of its
        own, whose handler finds the turn by id and splices it out.</p>

        <p>A <code>patch</code> mutates the value in place rather than minting a production (5.7),
        so a <code>schema</code> on the room never sees it. The check is on the way in: the
        <code>POST</code>'s <code>args</code> are derived from its annotation (19.9), and anything
        stricter goes in the handler beside the push.</p>

        <p>abide puts no cap on the array. The example trims in the same patch that appends —
        <code>if (turns.length &gt; 20) turns.shift()</code> — and the <code>shift</code> is one
        more op on the wire.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/change-something-on-the-server')}>Mutations</a> ·
        <a href={url('/app/know-who-is-calling')}>Auth &amp; principal</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Rungs on the `GET` decide who may follow">
        <p>A room anyone can follow is a room anyone can read. Put a rung on the
        <code>GET</code>:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`const room = GET(rooms, {
    middleware: [
        async (next, ctx) =>
            (await isMember(ctx.request, ctx.args())) ? next(ctx) : refuse(403),
    ],
})`} />

        <p>A read over http passes the rung like any call (16.11). A subscription runs it once, as
        it opens, in the request the connection opened in, and is sent productions only where the
        rung admitted it (18.8, 18.16). The decision is made per connection: access revoked after it
        opened takes effect when it next reconnects.</p>

        <p><code>crossOrigin</code> gates the same subscription from another origin, closed unless
        the <code>GET</code> admits that origin (18.2).</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/let-another-origin-call-you')}>CORS</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A follower is sent what a patch wrote, as JSON Patch">
        <p>A <code>patch</code> on the room is sent to every follower standing at the version it was
        made over as what it wrote, in JSON Patch (RFC 6902), and not as the array (18.11). Each
        write is a <code>PatchOp</code>: an <code>add</code>, <code>remove</code> or
        <code>replace</code> at a JSON Pointer, so a <code>push</code> is an <code>add</code> at
        <code>/-</code> and a <code>shift</code> a <code>remove</code> at <code>/0</code>. A
        twenty-turn room sends one turn per message, not twenty.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The patch</th><th>The follower is sent</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>over the version it holds</td><td>the ops</td></tr>
                <tr><td>over a version it does not hold</td><td>the ops, and it reads the value again (18.12)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>over a copy the page wrote to itself</td><td>the ops, and it reads the value again (18.21)</td></tr>
                <tr><td>one whose writes cannot be sent as data</td><td>the value, whole (18.13)</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A write to a followed copy stays in that tab — a <code>say</code> is how a turn reaches
        the room — and lasts until the server next changes the room.</p>

        <p>The same follow is open to anything that speaks http, from a handler file. The card's
        <code>room</code> is its own, at an address no listing names; to hand the room to a script,
        put the <code>GET</code> in <code>src/support/room.ts</code> as its default export, with
        <code>rooms</code> in a module under <code>server</code> that it and <code>say</code> both
        import. Ask <code>/support/room</code> for <code>text/event-stream</code> and it is
        server-sent events, each frame's op its <code>event</code>, its version the
        <code>id</code>, and the value or the JSON Patch its <code>data</code> (18.20):</p>

        <Code code={`curl -N -H 'Accept: text/event-stream' \\
  'https://app.example/support/room?id=lobby'`} />

        <p>An <code>EventSource</code> reconnecting sends the last <code>id</code> back as
        <code>Last-Event-ID</code>, and is sent only what moved after it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/ship/watch-a-room-from-the-terminal')}>Watch a channel</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A reconnect is sent the value, then its patches">
        <p>A version names one production or one patch, and no other in this process or in any
        earlier one (18.7). A client subscribing names the version it holds (18.9), and the server's
        answer follows from it (18.6):</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The client holds</th><th>It is sent</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>the value's current version</td><td>nothing, until the next patch</td></tr>
                <tr><td>any other version</td><td>the value, whole</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Seeding a first render, a connection that dropped and reopened, and a tab too slow to
        keep up are one mechanism, and none of it is spelled by the app.</p>

        <p>The value is not durable. It lives in memory, so a restart starts the room from its body
        again. History anyone must be able to scroll back past that is app data behind an ordinary
        rpc.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A followed value is still a reactive value">
        <p>The call hands back the same <code>Reactive</code> the handler had, <code>Produced</code>
        included (16.3). A bare read is the array, <code>room.at(-1)</code> is the latest turn, and
        the probes answer. A follower's copy is <code>busy()</code> until its first production
        arrives (9.14).</p>

        <p>Writing to that copy is not how to send. A <code>set</code> on it stands only until the
        next production replaces it (9.4), so a turn goes through <code>say</code>.</p>

        <p><code>{'room.invalidate({ id })'}</code> and <code>{'room.refresh({ id })'}</code> inside
        a handler reach every browser's copy of that room (7.10, 7.18). A <code>for await</code>
        over the room hands back the standing array once per turn, a burst collapsing to the latest
        (2.6), so code that has to act on every message acts in <code>say</code>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Each instance holds its own room">
        <p>A <code>global</code> value lives in the process, as its cache does. More than one
        instance degrades rather than breaks: a reconnect landing on another instance names a
        version that instance never minted, and is sent that instance's value whole. abide supplies
        no cross-process mechanism for it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/share-one-value-across-components')}>Sharing</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/values/let-anything-publish-and-anything-read')}>Channels</a> — what
            a channel is, before it has an address</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/read-data-without-writing-an-api')}>Reading data</a> —
            <code>GET</code>, and how any read follows the server</li>
            <li><a href={url('/ship/watch-a-room-from-the-terminal')}>Watch a channel</a> —
            following a <code>GET</code> from a shell</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
